 ## Overview
-Flat blocking multi-resolution volume rendering is a strategy for loading and rendering large volume datasets by dividing the volume into sub-blocks of equal physical size but assigning each block a different resolution (Level of Detail, LOD) based on its internal data variation [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of memory bandwidth and rendering time for large volumes by avoiding uniform high-resolution storage [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike hierarchical structures that change spatial extent, this method keeps block dimensions constant while varying the voxel count within them [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its explicit use of a "flat" blocking structure where LOD is determined per block via a variation heuristic, often implemented using min/max octrees [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Flat blocking multi-resolution volume rendering is a strategy for loading and rendering large volume datasets by dividing the volume into sub-blocks of equal physical size but assigning each block a different resolution (Level of Detail, LOD) based on its internal data variation [S1, S3, unofficial]. Unlike hierarchical structures that change spatial extent, this method keeps block dimensions constant while varying the voxel count within them [S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its explicit use of a "flat" blocking structure where LOD is determined per block via a variation heuristic, often implemented using min|max octrees [S1, S3, unofficial].
 
